1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,420
Welcome to CNS Pathology. The first section deals with developmental abnormalities.

2
00:00:04,980 --> 00:00:07,460
We're going to begin our discussion with neural tube defects.

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:15,600
Recall from embryology that we've got a neural plate, and this neural plate will invaginate in order to produce, eventually, a neural tube.

4
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:22,020
Now, of course, up here are the crests of this invagination, and these are called neural crests, and so they would be located here and here.

5
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:26,900
Important to note that the neural crests will go on to develop the peripheral nervous system.

6
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:35,620
The wall of the tube goes on to develop the central nervous system, and the lumen of the tube goes on to develop the ventricles and the spinal cord canal.

7
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:44,480
Now, recall from embryology that the neural tube forms from the cranial aspect of the fetus all the way to the caudal aspect, so from the head to the lower back.

8
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:52,520
And if there's a failure of closure of the neural tube, either at the cranial aspect or the caudal aspect, then that would result in a neural tube defect.

9
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:57,300
And so we say here in the text that these arise from incomplete closure of the neural tube.

10
00:00:57,740 --> 00:01:04,140
It's important to note that neural tube defects are associated with low folate levels, but, of course, the key time is prior to conception.

11
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:12,140
And so, if you want to protect a woman against neural tube defects, it's important that she have adequate folate levels prior to conception, high yield.

12
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:20,400
Neural tube defects can be detected during prenatal care, and this is via an elevated AFP in the amniotic fluid and in the maternal blood.

13
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:27,800
If the failure of closure occurs at the cranial aspect, we call that anencephaly, and it's characterized by absence of the skull and the brain.

14
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:34,160
Now, if the skull and the brain are missing, then the eyes become particularly prominent, and that results in a frog-like appearance of the fetus.

15
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:40,200
Here's an image of this, and you can clearly see that the skull and the brain, which should be above here, are clearly missing.

16
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:46,020
And so this results in a prominence of the eyes of the fetus, which then results in this frog-like appearance.

17
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:48,280
That's a very high-yield description.

18
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:54,500
Another very high-yield association with anencephaly is the fact that it results in maternal polyhydramnios.

19
00:01:55,100 --> 00:02:02,500
Recall from embryology that the baby floats around in amniotic fluid, and the amniotic fluid is generated by, predominantly, from the baby's urine.

20
00:02:03,060 --> 00:02:09,180
At the same time, the baby swallows some of the amniotic fluid to help decrease some of the volume, and then resorbs it.

21
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:14,800
Now, in anencephaly, because there's an absence of the brain, there's an absence of the CNS-controlled swallowing centers,

22
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:17,100
and so, therefore, the baby won't be able to swallow.

23
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:22,620
And if the baby can't swallow, that's going to result in increased amniotic fluid, or polyhydramnios.

24
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:25,500
And that, again, is a particularly high-yield association.

25
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:31,200
If there's failure of closure of the neural tube at the caudal aspect, we call that spina bifida,

26
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:35,640
and the hallmark is that you fail to close the posterior vertebral arch.

27
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:42,940
Now, this can simply result in a dimple or a patch of hair overlying that defect, and that's called spina bifida occulta.

28
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:48,620
Or it can result in the meninges, or the meninges plus the spinal cord, herniating through that defect,

29
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:50,780
and then that would be called outright spina bifida.

30
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:55,100
When it's the meninges alone that comes out of that defect, we call that a meningoseal.

31
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:59,640
When it's the meninges plus the spinal cord, we call that a meningomyeloseal.

32
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:07,220
Cerebral aqueduct stenosis is congenital stenosis of the channel that drains CSF from the third to fourth ventricles.

33
00:03:07,580 --> 00:03:12,680
Now, remember that within the ventricles, you have something called the choroid plexus, and that makes the CSF.

34
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:17,800
The CSF then flows from the lateral to the third ventricle, and that's via the foramen of Monroe,

35
00:03:18,220 --> 00:03:21,440
and then from the third to the fourth ventricle via the cerebral aqueduct.

36
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:28,180
And so if you have cerebral aqueduct stenosis, then you will not be able to drain CSF through the ventricular system,

37
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:31,300
and instead, CSF will accumulate within the ventricles.

38
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:35,020
This will result in enlargement of the ventricles, called hydrocephalus,

39
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:39,140
and because this is presenting in an infant, the head will then enlarge,

40
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:41,700
and this will present with an enlarging head circumference.

41
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:46,420
Dandy-Walker malformation is a congenital failure to develop the cerebellar vermis.

42
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:50,420
The cerebellar vermis separates the two sides of the cerebellum.

43
00:03:50,860 --> 00:03:55,660
It presents as a massively dilated fourth ventricle with absence of the cerebellum,

44
00:03:55,660 --> 00:03:58,060
often accompanied by hydrocephalus.

45
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:00,100
Here's a classic image, very high yield.

46
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:01,660
I like to show this on examinations.

47
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:08,300
You can clearly see that there is this massively dilated fourth ventricle with absence of most of the cerebellum.

48
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:14,040
Arnold-Chiari malformation is when you get congenital extension of the cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum.

49
00:04:14,380 --> 00:04:18,760
Just illustrating this very simplistically, if this hole represents the foramen magnum of the skull,

50
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:20,980
then the brainstem is actually coming through that.

51
00:04:20,980 --> 00:04:24,760
Now, of course, posterior to the brainstem here would be the cerebellum.

52
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:29,220
Now, imagine if the tonsils of the cerebellum extended through the foramen magnum.

53
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:31,340
That would then be an Arnold-Chiari malformation.

54
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:35,560
Now, Arnold-Chiari malformations can be divided into type 1 and type 2.

55
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:37,460
In type 1, there really are no symptoms.

56
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:40,440
However, in type 2, you can get obstruction of CSF flow.

57
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:42,340
Recall that the fourth ventricle is right here.

58
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:46,760
And so you can get some compression resulting in obstruction of the CSF flow.

59
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:50,420
So if there's obstruction of the CSF flow, that could result in hydrocephalus.

60
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:53,220
That's an important association with Arnold-Chiari malformations.

61
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:59,800
In addition, a very high-yield association is that you can also see meningomyeloceles with Arnold-Chiari malformation

62
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:03,960
along with a spinal cord lesion called syringomyelia.

63
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:06,800
And we'll talk about syringomyelia in just a few moments.

64
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:36,780
Thank you.

